.Dd February 11, 2026
.Dt PR 1
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm pr
.Nd paginate or columnate files for printing
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl h Ar header
.Op Fl l Ar lines
.Op Fl m
.Op Fl n Op Ar sep Ns Op Ar digits
.Op Fl s Op Ar char
.Op Fl t
.Op Fl w Ar width
.Op Fl Ar columns
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility formats text files into pages suitable for printing.
By default,
.Nm
prints 66-line pages with a date and page header.
.Pp
If no
.Ar file
operands are given,
or if a
.Ar file
operand is
.Sq - ,
.Nm
reads standard input.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
In multi-column mode, print columns across the page (row-major order)
instead of down the page.
.It Fl h Ar header
Use
.Ar header
instead of the file name in the page header.
.It Fl l Ar lines
Set page length to
.Ar lines .
Default is 66.
.It Fl m
Merge all input files side-by-side in parallel columns.
.It Fl n Op Ar sep Ns Op Ar digits
Enable line numbering.
The optional
.Ar sep
character separates the number from text
(default tab), and
.Ar digits
sets number field width (default 5).
.It Fl s Op Ar char
Use
.Ar char
as the inter-column separator.
If omitted, a tab is used.
.It Fl t
Suppress page headers and trailers.
.It Fl w Ar width
Set page width to
.Ar width
columns.
.It Fl Ar columns
Set number of output columns using legacy
.Fl 3 ,
.Fl 4 ,
and similar forms.
.El
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
This implementation follows POSIX
.Nm
behavior for core pagination and columns,
with UTF-8 display width handling using
.Xr wcwidth 3
for better multibyte alignment.
.Pp
To avoid conflicts with POSIX
.Fl n
line numbering,
column count is selected with the legacy
.Fl Ar columns
form.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Format a report with custom page length:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pr -l 50 -h "Report" file1 file2
.Ed
.Pp
Read from standard input:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cat input.txt | pr -w 100 -2
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nl 1 ,
.Xr paste 1 ,
.Xr wcwidth 3
